A||N||26||Hym-The-Man||Electric Water Pump||||1||
Z||000000||Hym-The-Man||02-01-2001||06:53 PM||||Does anyone have any experience with the electric water pumps out there.  Ther are supposed to make more hp by eliminating the strain on the engine that whe water pump causes.<BR>	Here is a link to one company that makes them.  I want to talk to a real  person that has used one. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.daviescraig.com.au/newproduct.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.daviescraig.com.au/newproduct.html</A> <P><BR> I am going to put a Aurora v8 32v in my fiero.  I chose the Aurora because of its light weight, sound, and gas mileage.  If I could get an extra 20 horses and 15 lb ft. of torque out of that thing, that would be sweet.||||reg||1
Z||000001||ray b||02-01-2001||08:27 PM||||N* uses pump on back of cam aurora same? elt. pump can fail and take out motor.N* makes 50 more hp same out side size as aurora.<BR>elt. pump used on chevy v8 for clearance of frame but many say do not use as most dont move enuff coolant fast enuff+ can fail and cook motor.N* or aurora  pump not in way in the fiero swap.   ray<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Will||02-02-2001||11:11 AM||||Going to an electric water pump won't free up any power. You'd just be shifting the load from the water pump drive to the alternator.<BR>Ray is right. Electric water pumps are used on SBC conversions to avoid cutting a hole in the wheel house.<BR>The N*/Aurora uses a waterpump mounted on the back of the left bank. That engine requires no adapter plate or modifications to the chassis, other than trimming the hood hinge.<BR>Do you already have an engine? I know a nearby junkyard which has a 45K mile engine for $1800.||||reg||1
Z||000003||BigTurbo87GT||02-02-2001||12:00 PM||||Actually, an electric water pump is usually better in just about every way except possibly service life (depending of course on quality of pump purchased). A belt driven pump usually has pretty poor impeller design because there's plenty of power to spin it and no real incentive to make it efficient. It also varies it's flow rate with rpm of engine, causing cavitation at high rpm and low flow at low rpm (any attempt to fix one condition exacerbates the other). As the rpm of the engine increases the drag induced by the belt driven pump increases.<P>The electric pump operates at a constant flow rate and has a well designed impeller to minimize the power necessary to operate the pump. Since the pump is running at a constant speed it must be sized appropriately for the application. The added draw on the alternator is minimal and is nowhere close to the parasitic draw of a belt powered water pump.<P>Depending on the application, you can free up about 7-10 HP.<P>Now, electric isn’t for everybody, especially if you do a lot of driving every day. They are not cheap, I spent 200 hundred for a descent pump from Mezier for my Z.<BR>Down side of electric is the dependable life expectancy of around 2500 hours, which is a pretty descent amount of time. And that is not to say it will die at 2500 hour mark, probably last longer but should be inspected regularly after this point.<BR>Assuming you spend 2 hours in your car EVERY DAY, in two years you would probably need to start thinking about replacing the water pump.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Clean stocker<BR>95 Z28, Clean not so stocker||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||02-02-2001||12:05 PM||||Thats not a long service life at all.. But i have a question. If the alt needs to put out more juice (for more accessories), does it actually become harder to spin or something?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||02-02-2001||03:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>Thats not a long service life at all.. But i have a question. If the alt needs to put out more juice (for more accessories), does it actually become harder to spin or something?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes. Power doesn't come from nowhere. If you want to put a 55 amp draw on the alternator (14V*55A=770W), you will need to turn it with just over 1 horsepower (739W/hp). That is the ideal situation. You will actually need to put more power in because of friction, resistive losses in the electronics, etc.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>Actually, an electric water pump is usually better in just about every way except possibly service life (depending of course on quality of pump purchased). A belt driven pump usually has pretty poor impeller design because there's plenty of power to spin it and no real incentive to make it efficient. It also varies it's flow rate with rpm of engine, causing cavitation at high rpm and low flow at low rpm (any attempt to fix one condition exacerbates the other). As the rpm of the engine increases the drag induced by the belt driven pump increases.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's actually beneficial that a water pump pumps more water at higher RPM than at low RPM. This means that there is maximum flow through the cooling system when the engine is producing maximum heat. When the engine is at low RPM producing little heat, the parsitic loss of the pump is smaller. The parasitic loss of an electric water pump is constant. And if the pump has enough capacity to cool the engine at high RPM, its using much more power than it has to at low RPM.<P>Getting sufficient capacity in an electric water pump is another trick.<BR>Eric Marsh, on this forum, has a 406 SBC in his 88 GT. He used a Moroso electric water pump at first, only to find that it has inadequate capacity.<P>I'd also say that the electric pump is at a HUGE disadvantage for service life. My 87 GT still has its original water pump after 13 years and 120K miles.<P>No reason to make a belt driven pump efficient? What planet are you from? The EPA is breathing down the necks of all auto manufacturers. Don't you think they'd sell their own mothers for an extra 1/10th point of CAFE?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||02-02-2001||08:02 PM||||Well.... to make this even more interesting... you all might want to look at the 36/42v systems comming. These systems will run all kinds of things electrically. The water pump and power steering will both likely be electric.<P>For a 12/14v system...  (bat volt/charge volt)<P>Life of an electric water pump depends entirely on who made it and just what they made it for. Many of these pumps are made for race applications. There are pumps made that will last in street use. They just may be hard to find.<P>Don't assume electric pumps turn at constant velocity. There are several smart controllers for these pumps that vary speed based on engine temperature. These controllers do away with the thermostat as well as the belt driven pump.||||reg||1
Z||000007||bubbajoexx||02-02-2001||09:18 PM||||<P><BR> If I could get an extra 20 horses and 15 lb ft. of torque out of that thing, that would be sweet.[/B][/QUOTE]<P>the Aurora V8 is 4 litre 240 hp 290ftlbs of torque  I hope you are ready to remove 300 lines of code from the Aurora computer if you want the engine to run properly GOOD LUCK||||reg||1
Z||000008||Hym-The-Man||02-02-2001||10:26 PM||||Thanks for all of the info.  I know that this will always be a touchy subject.  I think that I will wait and see until more research is done on them.  <BR>	Here is another question.  I don't understand why so many people are against putting a Northstar or Aurora v8 into a Fiero?  I thought that we are supposd to all be here to help one another.  I realize that it will be a tough thing to do, but I want to do it.  That is what it boils down to.  <BR>	If I want to pull all of my hair out trying to shoehorn a v8 into a shoebox then that should be enough for everyone.<BR>	I am not pointing to anything specific,or anyone in particular.  I have read alot of the posts here about the northstar, and many have been helpful, but some people are afraid of things because they don't have the balls or money to do it.<BR>	Well I am a crazy person, I love to do things that are new.  I love the idea of a car that would take a mustang off the line and kick its ass in the quarter for alot less, or even run close with a ferrari 355, Lamborghini Diablo, or Dodge viper for tons less money.  The best part of the whole thing is that it would be built by me.  I love self gratification.<BR>	When I do the swap I will post all pictures of the process and all information so that anyone else that is interested in the swap can have a point of reference.  It is all about the brotherhood. <BR>	On that note if anyone has any info about the swap that they want to pass along to me that would be great.<P>Thanks guys.<P>I hope that this didn't sound mean.  That was not my intention<BR>	||||reg||1
Z||000009||ray b||02-02-2001||11:11 PM||||chrfab [http//wwwchrfab.comindex.htm] for a start I like carbs.plus no computer to f with .they have spark controler as stand alone unit.<BR>But unsure about trans no auto but 80e rated for tork and 80e elct controled nitemare to use.ray||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||02-02-2001||11:16 PM||||I'm doing this swap too, I just don't have a lot of time or cash flow right now.<BR>I posted a while back about the mechanical nature of a Northstar swap. Use the search function. That post might be in the archives by now.<P>The electronics are not that bad.<BR>BubbaJoexx is flat wrong. I have looked through the shop manuals for both N* and Aurora. The Aurora chip will run the Cadillac engine with a manual transmission and no traction control without serious complaints. The only thing you have to do is turn up the fuel pressure on the N*, but that is no sweat because the engine is equipped with an adjustable fuel pressure regulator from the factory.<BR>Although I haven't tested it yet, I am also fairly sure that an LS1/LS6 controller can run a N*, although it will need to be remapped to run it well.<P>The Aurora produces 250 hp and 260 ftlbs of torque. <A HREF="http://www.auroracar.com/section/Performance.asp" TARGET=_blank>http://www.auroracar.com/section/Performance.asp</A> <P>I think that the biggest reasons Northstar swaps are not done more than they are is fear and ingorance.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Standard||02-04-2001||02:28 AM||||Mainly 'cause they're pretty expensive... and  for less cost and less headache you can throw a 3800 Series II s/c with 240 hp and 275 ft/lb.. add a smaller pulley to up the boost and get some cheap horsepower.<P>The northstar engine sure does look right at home in the Fiero's engine bay, though.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/northstargt1_small.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 02-04-2001).]||||reg||
Z||000012||Will||02-04-2001||11:01 AM||||How much does an SBC conversion cost? How much does a 3800 conversion cost?<BR>I estimate that I will have spent $4000 to $4500 on the swap by the time I have finished. That is doing all the work myself and INCLUDES the price of the car.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Old Lar||02-04-2001||01:10 PM||||Cost depends on who does it. If you are farming out all the work.  A 3.4 is $4500, a 3800 sc  is $8000-10000,  a N* is $12000+. Including engine.<P>Just dropping the engine in is only the beginning.  You'll need suspension upgrades, transmission upgrades and brake upgrade to have a reliable driving car.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000014||Hym-The-Man||02-04-2001||04:53 PM||||I understand that the swap will be difficult. I think that I have all of the bases covered. See what you guys think.<P>Suspension will be handled by: <A HREF="http://www.shelbycustom.com/addons/addons.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.shelbycustom.com/addons/addons.html</A> <P>Brakes will be handled by: <A HREF="http://www.ipass.net/~fieroman/terranexus//fieroman/walt/walt.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ipass.net/~fieroman/terranexus//fieroman/walt/walt.htm</A> <P>Engine Management will be handled by: <A HREF="http://www.chrfab.com/Ignitions.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.chrfab.com/Ignitions.htm</A> <P>Shifting will be handled by an Aurora standard tranny or modified Getrag, although I wish that I could find a 6-speed tranny for the thing.<P>Bigger radiator, Higher volume fuel pump, Larger master cylinder, Mr. Mikes Leather seat covers style 430,Seat heaters for cold winter days by:http://home.flash.net/~duetto/general/genseatheaters.htm <P>I am also going to have the heads ported and polished by chrfab for an extra 20 Hp.  My friend works in a muffler shop so I have the exhaust covered.<BR>	<BR>I am also going to reinforce the chasis first of all to add alittle weight to the front and to strengthen it overall.<P>I am wishing that someone had information on doing a anti-lock brake conversion.<P><BR>So what do you think?  Let me know what suggeston you guys have.<P>I think that I am going to start a new post with this as the topic||||reg||1
