Z||000017||Hym-The-Man||02-04-2001||06:59 PM||||Sorry to be dumb here, but whatis an sci?||||reg||1
Z||000018||Will||02-04-2001||07:16 PM||||Hym-the-man:<P>What radiator will you be using?<BR>The 94-96 Corvette fuel pump is a plug in swap with no mods required.<P>What chassis reinforcements are you going to be doing?<P>A good way to shift weight to the front is by putting the battery under the spare tire.<P>If you REALLY want to lay out some cash and do some development, <A HREF="http://www.stealthracing.co.uk" TARGET=_blank>www.stealthracing.co.uk</A>  sells a 6 speed kit for VW 02A transaxle which is supposed to be able to handle 400+ ftlbs. Adapting this trans to the Fiero would take a good bit of work, however.<P>Why are you using aftermarket engine marangement? That adds a LOT to the cost of the conversion. The stock Aurora electronics should run the engine with a manual trans. They will however set trouble codes and light the check engine light. You can sort through this with a diagnostic scanner, which you should DEFINITELY have if your are even thinking about this swap.<P>I have been musing for a while about converting the Fiero to F-body front hub bearings (and 5x115 bolt circle) in the front and Caddilac front hub bearings in the rear. The idea behind this is to have the more prevalent large bolt circle, and to have F-body and Caddy wheel speed sensors. A Corvette four channel ABS controller could then be used to retrofit ABS. I don't know if the Corvette controller is stand-alone or not, though.<P>Initially, you should only need polyurethane control arm and cradle bushings and a rear anti-swaybar. Coil overs are not necessary for the swap.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Hym-The-Man||02-04-2001||10:01 PM||||As far as strengthening the chasis I am going to use the technique posted here: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007503.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007503.html</A> <P>Plus a few modifications around the front and rear firewalls for torsional stiffness.  I want to do a 355 spyder kit, and this would allow me to do the frame stretch and strengthen all at the same time.<P>As far as the engine management I would really like to use the Aurora ecm, but I have been scared by all of the posts concerning how difficult it would be to use it.<P>There is an advantage to using it that I just thought of.  I could use the wheel sensors from a Aurora and maybe the ecm could handle the abs.  That sounds so demanding though. <P>If anyone has any ideas here I would be very grateful.||||reg||1
Z||000020||ray b||02-04-2001||11:26 PM||||i wonder if older irl majic boxes [eltric control] could be used, older race stuff sometimes cheap if no longer used on cars.<BR>anti lock good idea older one may stand alone.newer gran am than 90 have them?capliper will it fit bolt on?<BR>pre obd-2 pre 95 stock chip may work ok if aurora obd-1 FI used.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Chris McDonald||02-06-2001||01:32 PM||||Hym-The-Man,<P>Man, you kill me.  You are asking for input from others but taking a negative attitude to those that have taken their valuable time to point out the realistic challenges you will face while doing such a conversion.  It does not mean anybody is against you. Rather, they have a legitimate concern to help point you down the right path.<P>With that said, I must say that there is no way you are going to complete this job with the items you mentioned for $4,500. More like $14,500. <P>I speak to you from experience.  I am also a die hard do it yourselfer.  Much like you I have charged into a number of projects against the good advice of others.  That is why I have a feeling you are really going to try this.<P>My most recent challeng is putting the finishing touches on a 1995 LT-1 swap into a 1989 1500 Chevy truck.  We are pushing $6,000 in this current job counting the cost of the motor/trans, accessories, harness, computer, and a large number of other components like headers, exhaust work, and detail.  That price does not include the original cost of the truck and no adapter plate or custom work needed to bolt it down. Not to mention I am also having significant progaming problems getting the engine to run right in its new home. <P>I had over $12,000 in my Carburated V8 Fiero doing 99.9% of the work myself.  Oh yah, plenty of people tried to talk me out of that.<P><BR>Good luck, and be kind to strangers willing to give free advice.  ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Hym-The-Man||02-06-2001||05:53 PM||||Chris McDonald<P>	Hello Chris.  I am sorry for coming off as a mean person.  As I said at the end of the post that is not my intention.  I am truly asking this brain trust of Fiero owners what they think.  I know that some of the people on this fourm have done what I plan on doing.  Therefore this is the greatest place for info.   Plus this a technical section not a "hey that is a stupid idea" secton.  I would think that the idea is stupid if no one had ever done it. People on this very forum have done the conversion and I am begging them for their ideas, experience, feedback, questions, comments etc. I NEED HELP!  And please everyone that has posted here I thank you for the information that you have given.  It has been helpful.  Much Love<BR>	That said, I never mentioned a price on my conversion, I believe that was Will.$4,000-$4500.  I wish him luck and hope that he is right on the money.  I have no idea what mine will cost.  I am looking into engines and actually fould on about 20mi from me that had a compression test done and everything is good.  The motor will cost me $1,020 and it only has 22,000 miles on it. <BR>	I know that this will be a long arduous task, and it will probably take every penny that i think about for a long while.  I am even picking up pennies off the street to help finance it.<BR>	I wish you good luck on your truck project<BR>	||||reg||1
Z||000023||Will||02-06-2001||06:27 PM||||Chris:<P>Do you know about LT1 Edit? <A HREF="http://www.carputing.com" TARGET=_blank>www.carputing.com</A>  My dad and I will be getting a copy of that software shortly.<P><BR>Hym:<P>$1020 for a 22K engine? That's a deal. You won't find many more at that price.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Chris McDonald||02-09-2001||04:17 PM||||No, I am not sure I know about that.  We are on planning on pulling the computer this weekend and shipping to Street and Performance for tuning.  Turns out the computer was out of a RWD Cadilac with the LT-1 detuned engine like the Impala SS.  It is the same basic box but has a way different program.  In fact, it shuts down at 98 mph.  With 4:10 gears in this truck that is around 60 mph.  That has been driving us CRAZY.  <P>Anyway, S&P will reprogram the shift patterns, fuel, timing, etc, and REMOVE the speed limiter.  We should be rocking soon.  However, I will check on that link you posted.  Who knows, it might do the trick.<P>Thanks,<P>Chris<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||Chris McDonald||02-09-2001||04:19 PM||||No problem.  Now that I think about it, I was in a pretty bad mood the other day.  <P>I am sure you can pull this off.  It is not going to come easy and you will have many battle stories to tell when it is done.  Using that kind of engine limits the amount of help you will be able to get at reasonable prices.  <P>Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000026||Hym-The-Man||02-09-2001||06:24 PM||||If I can pull this off I am going to share all info about the conversion.  I want there to be hundreds of Fiero oweners out there smoking the competition!   For example I can get a honda civic to run a 12 or 13 sec qr. for about $4-8000.00 plus the cost of the civic at $15-22,000 adding up to $19-30,000. But you can find an old junker Fiero,(lets face it there are lots of people that just drove them into the ground), for a few hundred bucks. You can easily get 330 hp from the N* by porting the heads and intake manifold, in conjunction with a 3" exaust. Total cost on the N* $4-6000 plus the mods to the trany, brakes, suspension,etc you are looking at another $4-6000. Now the N* would come from a salvage yard and I would suggest a through testing of the engine before you purchase it. I would suggest goint to: <A HREF="http://www.car-part.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.car-part.com</A> <BR>They have a nationwide search on all parts you could need.<BR>Plus other odds-and-ends you are looking at another $2-4000.  That is if you do all of the work yourself. and for $10-16,000 yuo can have a super fast ride that is All-American.<BR>	I may be optimistic about the costs, but I cant be too far off.  If I am please tell me so<BR>||||reg||1
